---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import portrait from '../assets/portrait.jpg';

const legendLinks = {
  salab: 'https://salabufmg.github.io/',
  fame: 'https://salabufmg.github.io/FAME26/',
  mlsa: 'https://dtai.cs.kuleuven.be/events/MLSA26/index.php',
};
const a = 'text-[var(--acc)] no-underline border-b border-[var(--acc)]/45 hover:border-[var(--acc)]';
---
<!-- Two columns from 56rem (896px) up, one below. NOT md: (768px). Base.astro's
     container is max-w-4xl + px-6, so content only reaches its full 848px at an 896px
     viewport; 896 is precisely where max-w-4xl stops growing and the text column locks
     at its permanent 460px. Splitting at 768 instead handed the text 332–398px while
     the portrait kept a fixed 340px, which is the whole tablet band — iPad 9th (768),
     10th (810), Air (820), Pro 11" (834) all portrait. Measured across 767 -> 768, a ONE
     PIXEL change of viewport: the text column fell 719px -> 332px, the lead's measure
     fell 576.5px -> 332px, the h1 gained two one-word orphan lines ("department." and
     "conference." each alone), and the hero collapsed 1059.1px -> 840.7px. max-w-[23ch]
     on the h1 (457.3px) and max-w-[56ch] on the lead (565.3px) were both inert from 768
     to 895, because the column was narrower than either cap.
     On a phone the grid collapses and the figure lands after the identity block: the
     h1 is the strongest claim on the site and has to be the first thing on a small
     screen, ahead of the portrait. -->
<section data-testid="hero" class="grid gap-10 py-16 min-[56rem]:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_auto] min-[56rem]:items-start min-[56rem]:gap-12">
  <div>
    <!-- text-balance on each sentence. Both halves of this are measured, in Chromium and
         WebKit, on the built page with the fonts loaded — an earlier version of this
         comment claimed the balanced wrap held "at every width" and that was false at 768
         and 810 for as long as the hero split at md:.

         Without text-balance (measured by deleting it and rebuilding): at 414, 768, 820,
         896, 1280 and 1440 all three sentences land a single word on their second line —
         "department.", "lab.", "conference." — one of them the four-character "lab."

         With it: at 375, 390, 414, 612, 613, 767, 768, 810, 820, 834, 895, 896, 1024,
         1280 and 1440 every sentence occupies exactly two lines and carries at least two
         words onto the second, S1 breaking "Brazil's first club / analytics department."
         identically at all fifteen. That holds down to a 312px text column (viewport 360);
         below it — measured at 320, 330, 340, 350 — the column is too narrow for the
         balanced break and S1 goes to three lines ending on "department." No claim is
         made here about widths under 360. -->
    <h1 class="mb-6 max-w-[23ch] font-serif text-[clamp(1.9rem,5vw,2.35rem)] font-normal leading-[1.24] tracking-[-0.022em]">
      <span class="block text-balance">Brazil's <span class="highlight">first</span> club analytics department.</span>
      <span class="block text-balance">Its <span class="highlight">first</span> sports analytics lab.</span>
      <span class="block text-balance">Its <span class="highlight">first</span> football analytics conference.</span>
    </h1>

    <p class="mb-8 max-w-[56ch] text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.66] text-[var(--dim)]">
      I've spent my career building the parts of a football operation that didn't exist yet —
      <b class="font-semibold text-[var(--ink)]">a department, a research lab, a conference now in its fifth edition</b>
      — and then running them. These days I lead data recruitment at RSC Anderlecht.
    </p>

    <div class="max-w-[56ch] border-t-2 border-[var(--ink)] pt-4">
      <div class="mb-1 text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">Hugo Rios-Neto</div>
      <!-- Separators use --faint, not --hair2: the latter is 1.39:1 on cream, effectively
           invisible, and these are the only marks dividing the three role clauses.
           aria-label on each link because a screen-reader user listing links otherwise
           hears three bare acronyms with no role and no expansion — the visible
           "Co-founder," is not part of the accessible name.
           Each aria-label OPENS with the link's exact visible text, then expands it.
           That ordering is WCAG 2.5.3 Label in Name (A): a speech-input user says what
           they can read on screen, so the visible string has to appear in the
           accessible name. Do not reorder these to lead with the expansion.
           Non-breaking hyphens in Co&#8209;founder and Co&#8209;organizer: both were
           breaking after the hyphen, leaving a line ending "Co-". -->
      <p data-testid="legend" class="text-sm leading-[1.7] text-[var(--dim)]">
        Data Recruitment Lead, RSC Anderlecht
        <span class="mx-2 text-[var(--faint)]" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
        Co&#8209;founder, <a class={a} href={legendLinks.salab} target="_blank" rel="noopener"
           aria-label="SALab — Sports Analytics Lab at UFMG">SALab</a>
        &amp; <a class={a} href={legendLinks.fame} target="_blank" rel="noopener"
           aria-label="FAME — Football Analytics: Modeling and Experience">FAME</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-[var(--faint)]" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
        Co&#8209;organizer, <a class={a} href={legendLinks.mlsa} target="_blank" rel="noopener"
           aria-label="MLSA @ ECML/PKDD — Machine Learning and Data Mining for Sports Analytics">MLSA @ ECML/PKDD</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- eager + fetchpriority="high", never loading="lazy": on desktop this sits above
       the fold and is the LCP element, so a lazy load would defer the very paint
       Lighthouse measures.
       widths + sizes rather than densities: densities emits 1x/2x descriptors, which
       force every viewport to reason from the desktop candidate.
       The astro:assets import carries the intrinsic 1260x1575, so width/height ship in
       the markup and the box is reserved before the bytes arrive — no layout shift.

       Below 56rem the portrait is w-full capped at max-w-[56ch] — the SAME cap the lead
       paragraph and the identity block already carry, so the photo's left and right
       edges land on exactly the same two verticals as the rule above it at every width
       below the breakpoint. It was previously pinned to 280px while that rule ran to
       327px (at 375), 366px (at 414) and 565px (at 767), so its right edge aligned to
       nothing and it read as a thumbnail that had failed to load. 56ch measures 565.3px.

       The sizes list tracks those CSS boundaries exactly, or the tablet band downloads
       the wrong candidate: 340px from 896 up; 565px from 613 (where the 56ch cap starts
       binding — 565.3 + 48px of px-6 gutters) to 895; viewport minus the gutters below
       that. 1130 = 565 at 2x, 680 = 340 at 2x. There is DELIBERATELY no candidate above
       1130: a DPR3 phone then takes the largest available rather than pulling a 3x
       asset. That is the standard 2x-ceiling trade and it is what keeps the bytes sane —
       do not "complete" the list with a 1695.

       The border is load-bearing, not decoration: the subject wears cream against a
       cream wall and the page background is cream, so without a defined edge the
       photograph has no boundary. It uses --frame (2.62:1 on --bg), not --cardline,
       which measures 1.18:1 — a border that asserted an edge without drawing one, and
       left the photo's pale top corners visibly dissolving into the page. -->
  <figure class="m-0">
    <Image src={portrait}
           alt="Hugo Rios-Neto at RSC Anderlecht's honours wall."
           widths={[280, 340, 400, 565, 680, 800, 1130]}
           sizes="(min-width: 896px) 340px, (min-width: 613px) 565px, calc(100vw - 3rem)"
           loading="eager"
           fetchpriority="high"
           class="w-full max-w-[56ch] rounded-xl border border-[var(--frame)] min-[56rem]:w-[340px]" />
  </figure>
</section>
